# 🔗 Logical & Bitwise Operators in Java

> These operators are used to perform operations on **boolean values** and **bits (binary level)**.

---

# 🧠 1. Logical Operators (Boolean Level)

Used when working with **true / false conditions**.

Assume:

```java id="a1"
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
```

| Operator | Name | Example  | Result |    |   |    |      |
| -------- | ---- | -------- | ------ | -- | - | -- | ---- |
| `&&`     | AND  | `a && b` | false  |    |   |    |      |
| `        |      | `        | OR     | `a |   | b` | true |
| `!`      | NOT  | `!a`     | false  |    |   |    |      |

---

## ⚙️ Explanation

### 🔗 AND (`&&`)

* True only if **both conditions are true**

```java id="a2"
true && true  // true
true && false // false
```

---

### 🔀 OR (`||`)

* True if **at least one condition is true**

```java id="a3"
true || false // true
false || false // false
```

---

### 🔄 NOT (`!`)

* Reverses the value

```java id="a4"
!true  // false
!false // true
```

---

## ⚠️ Important Concept: Short-Circuit

* `&&` and `||` use **short-circuit evaluation**

```java id="a5"
if (false && (10 / 0 > 1)) {
    // second condition will NOT execute
}
```

👉 Improves performance + avoids errors

