# ⚙️ 2. Bitwise Operators (Binary Level)

Work on **bits (0s and 1s)**

Assume:

```java id="a6"
int x = 5;  // 0101
int y = 3;  // 0011
```

---

## 🔢 Bitwise Operators

| Operator | Name | Example | Result |    |   |
| -------- | ---- | ------- | ------ | -- | - |
| `&`      | AND  | `x & y` | 1      |    |   |
| `        | `    | OR      | `x     | y` | 7 |
| `^`      | XOR  | `x ^ y` | 6      |    |   |
| `~`      | NOT  | `~x`    | -6     |    |   |

---

## ⚙️ Explanation

### 🔗 AND (`&`)

* Bit is 1 only if **both bits are 1**

```
0101
0011
----
0001 → 1
```

---

### 🔀 OR (`|`)

* Bit is 1 if **any one bit is 1**

```
0101
0011
----
0111 → 7
```

---

### 🔁 XOR (`^`)

* Bit is 1 if **bits are different**

```
0101
0011
----
0110 → 6
```

---

### 🔄 XNOR (Not Direct in Java ❗)

* XNOR = opposite of XOR
* Formula:

```java id="a7"
int result = ~(x ^ y);
```

---

# 🔄 3. Shift Operators

Used to shift bits left or right

---

## 📊 Types of Shift Operators

| Operator | Name           | Example   |
| -------- | -------------- | --------- |
| `<<`     | Left Shift     | `x << 1`  |
| `>>`     | Right Shift    | `x >> 1`  |
| `>>>`    | Unsigned Right | `x >>> 1` |

---

## ⚙️ Explanation

### ⬅️ Left Shift (`<<`)

* Shifts bits to left
* Multiplies by 2 (approx)

```java id="a8"
int x = 5;   // 0101
x << 1;      // 1010 → 10
```

---

### ➡️ Right Shift (`>>`)

* Shifts bits to right
* Divides by 2 (approx)

```java id="a9"
int x = 5;   // 0101
x >> 1;      // 0010 → 2
```

---

### ⚡ Unsigned Right Shift (`>>>`)

* Fills left bits with 0 (no sign bit)

```java id="a10"
int x = -5;
x >>> 1;
```

---

# ⚠️ Important Points

* Logical operators → work on **boolean**
* Bitwise operators → work on **integers (bits)**
* XOR is very useful in:

  * Swapping numbers
  * Finding unique elements

---

## 🚀 Quick Example

```java id="a11"
class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Logical
        System.out.println(true && false); // false
        System.out.println(true || false); // true
        System.out.println(!true);         // false

        // Bitwise
        int x = 5, y = 3;
        System.out.println(x & y); // 1
        System.out.println(x | y); // 7
        System.out.println(x ^ y); // 6

        // Shift
        System.out.println(x << 1); // 10
        System.out.println(x >> 1); // 2
    }
}
```

---

# ✅ Summary

* Logical operators → `&&`, `||`, `!` (boolean logic)
* Bitwise operators → `&`, `|`, `^`, `~` (binary operations)
* XNOR → done using `~(x ^ y)`
* Shift operators → `<<`, `>>`, `>>>`
* Important for **conditions + low-level logic**

